  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "Gold competes with cash and bonds. When bonds pay less after inflation, holding gold gives up less income. Prices tend to rise when spending, including credit, grows faster than the goods and services available to buy.",
   "data": "Three forces matter. The latest real yield dipped to 2.64%, easing the cost of holding gold at the margin. The breakeven fell to 2.24%, suggesting less expected inflation. Claims fell to 189,000, a sign layoffs remained low that complicates the case for easier policy. Real yields remain positive and the latest reading predates the sell-off, so the move does not establish a switch to easy money.",
   "unsustainable": "When debt grows persistently faster than income, servicing it absorbs more receipts. Spending restraint, restructuring, transfers or monetary easing can shift that burden.",
