   "cycle": "Higher debt relative to revenue and slower growth in foreign Treasury holdings point to a highly indebted phase. A family with a large interest bill has less cash for other expenses even if its income still grows. Here, the yield-growth gaps are still negative on the reported inputs, and the interest share combines different quarters. That is a narrower claim than saying the government already faces an immediate funding crisis.",
   "asset_classes": "Positive after-inflation cash and bond yields raise the cost of holding gold. If policy later lowers those real returns to relieve debt pressure, gold can benefit through that channel. Stocks depend on whether productivity and profits grow enough to offset financing costs. Falling yields during a sell-off can reflect weaker expectations for earnings, so the same change can have different effects on bonds and stocks."
